WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Piano instrumental

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.084
*phone rings*

00:00:05.084 --> 00:00:07.084
Here's something you hear a lot:

00:00:07.084 --> 00:00:12.917
"Say what you will about capitalism, you can't deny it's lifted people out of poverty"

00:00:12.917 --> 00:00:17.750
I hear that all the time, especially when i'm talking about rising inequality

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:19.750
and the vanishing middle class.

00:00:20.584 --> 00:00:23.334
The rates of extreme poverty, we are told,

00:00:23.334 --> 00:00:26.167
have plummeted under global capitalism.

00:00:26.584 --> 00:00:30.000
We are told that global&nbsp;capitalism has cut poverty by

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.417
as much as 80% or 90%.

00:00:32.417 --> 00:00:34.834
This reminds me of my mother.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:38.500
Whenever I ranted about how&nbsp;bad the situation was today,

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.298
she would tell me how much&nbsp;worse it had been before.

00:00:41.298 --> 00:00:45.084
"Be grateful for the improvements, Richard" she would say.

00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:48.167
I wasn't convinced then, and I'm not now.

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:52.084
These champions of capitalism&nbsp;are slick, I'll give them that,

00:00:52.084 --> 00:00:53.834
but here's why they're wrong.

00:00:54.334 --> 00:00:57.417
Their clever math comes from&nbsp;how they define poverty.

00:00:57.917 --> 00:01:04.917
According to the world bank, anyone making under $1.90 a day is considered "poor."

00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:07.084
Everyone else is okay!

00:01:07.084 --> 00:01:12.750
But that number isn't actually based on the living costs of any actual person.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:14.430
It's totally arbitrary!

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:17.734
Could YOU live on $1.90 a day?

00:01:17.734 --> 00:01:19.734
The United Nations doesn't think so.

00:01:20.417 --> 00:01:23.667
They say everyone would&nbsp;need at least 4x times that,

00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:28.500
$7.40 a day, to get the absolute minimum for

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:34.834
"basic nutrition and normal&nbsp;human life expectancy."

00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:39.334
Using this more realistic number,&nbsp;the number of people in poverty has

00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:41.970
actually increased over the last four decades,

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.121
to a whopping 4.2 billion.

00:01:45.121 --> 00:01:48.167
Poverty has gone up, not down.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:53.834
"But hey, at least a proportion of people&nbsp;in poverty has gone down," they might say.

00:01:54.417 --> 00:01:56.500
But what's actually the reason for that?

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:01.250
Was it REALLY capitalism that&nbsp;was&nbsp;our knight in shining armor?

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:07.250
As a matter of fact, almost all of that&nbsp;reduction in poverty has been in one country:

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.299
China.

00:02:08.299 --> 00:02:13.084
Not in the countries where&nbsp;American-style&nbsp;capitalism was exported.

00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:15.500
China accomplished this with

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.417
massive government industrial spending

00:02:18.417 --> 00:02:23.000
and social programs that it calls "socialist."

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.571
With a few exceptions, the rest of the world has&nbsp;seen very little progress in reducing poverty.

00:02:28.834 --> 00:02:33.834
If you exclude China, then not only has the number of people in poverty increased,

00:02:33.834 --> 00:02:37.784
but the proportion of people in&nbsp;poverty hasn't fared much better,

00:02:37.784 --> 00:02:42.583
ticking down a measly 5.4% over 40 years.

00:02:42.583 --> 00:02:50.702
At that rate, experts project&nbsp;it would take more than 200 years to come close to eliminating poverty.

00:02:50.702 --> 00:02:53.811
And that's only if there are never any recessions,

00:02:53.811 --> 00:02:59.741
and the global economy manages&nbsp;to&nbsp;reach 173x its current size.

00:02:59.741 --> 00:03:04.084
No one expects either of those things to happen.

00:03:04.084 --> 00:03:08.100
Already, the crisis caused&nbsp;&nbsp;by the Coronavirus pandemic 

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:12.000
has made years worth&nbsp;of&nbsp;poverty reduction worthless.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.921
100 million people have fallen right back into extreme poverty.

00:03:17.921 --> 00:03:22.084
But the problem isn't the&nbsp;viruses, viruses come and go.

00:03:22.084 --> 00:03:24.250
The real problem is *the system,*

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:27.280
a system that implodes every four to seven years,

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.544
pushing more and more people into deep poverty,

00:03:30.544 --> 00:03:34.694
and which has absolutely&nbsp;no&nbsp;protections for ordinary people

00:03:34.694 --> 00:03:36.005
when it collapses.

00:03:37.334 --> 00:03:41.917
Even the defenders of the system have started to admit just how flawed it is.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:48.167
The world bank itself now says&nbsp;that&nbsp;reducing inequality by just 1% a year

00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:50.584
would lift more people out of poverty

00:03:50.584 --> 00:03:55.142
than increasing economic growth&nbsp;by&nbsp;1% above current forecasts.

00:03:55.142 --> 00:03:58.782
That's not to say some&nbsp;things&nbsp;haven't gotten better,

00:03:58.782 --> 00:04:03.419
But where they are, it's because&nbsp;of&nbsp;ambitious government programs.

00:04:03.584 --> 00:04:08.917
That's why Cuba, an avowedly socialist country under a decades-long embargo,

00:04:08.917 --> 00:04:11.485
has a lower rate of infant mortality,

00:04:11.485 --> 00:04:13.779
a higher life expectancy,

00:04:13.779 --> 00:04:15.897
and a higher rate of literacy

00:04:15.897 --> 00:04:18.667
than the much wealthier United States.

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:22.084
And that's also why the countries&nbsp;with the lowest poverty are

00:04:22.084 --> 00:04:24.599
places like Denmark and Finland.

00:04:24.599 --> 00:04:26.199
It's no mystery:

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:30.167
no advanced economy has ever&nbsp;achieved low poverty rates

00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:35.845
without high levels of government social spending.

00:04:35.845 --> 00:04:41.584
And the fact is, capitalists have&nbsp;always fought against those policies.

00:04:41.584 --> 00:04:44.084
Against progressive taxation.

00:04:44.084 --> 00:04:46.209
Against minimum wages.

00:04:46.209 --> 00:04:49.054
Against social welfare programs.

00:04:49.054 --> 00:04:51.261
Against union bargaining.

00:04:51.261 --> 00:04:55.058
Poverty has been reduced not by capitalism,

00:04:55.058 --> 00:04:59.660
but in spite of and against&nbsp;capitalists' opposition.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.250
So it's not just that&nbsp;capitalism doesn't fix poverty, 

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.334
it plunges more and more people into desperation,

00:05:06.334 --> 00:05:08.654
so that the system can survive.

00:05:08.654 --> 00:05:12.544
The rising number of people&nbsp;in poverty can attest to that.

00:05:12.544 --> 00:05:16.586
And despite what the inaccurate statistics might suggest,

00:05:16.586 --> 00:05:20.280
as long as we have a system&nbsp;designed around profit,

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.310
That won't change.

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:24.115
*phone rings*

00:05:24.115 --> 00:05:27.354
This is Richard Wolff, professor of economics

00:05:27.354 --> 00:05:31.405
at the New School University, for the Gravel Institute.

00:05:31.405 --> 00:05:32.936
*phone distantly continues ringing*

00:05:32.936 --> 00:06:02.469
[techno music plays]

